@*
    OdsRecordCard — THE expandable record card behind every record list (Odyssey Design
    System · components/RecordCard, components/record-card-rules.html).

    Accounts, Contracts, Insurance policies, Subscriptions, Tax statements, Budgets,
    Journal entries and Exchange rates all render their rows through this one component.
    A dense, scannable header that expands a body in place. It is the product's ONLY
    record-detail surface — there are no record pages — so a body carries whatever the
    record needs: charts, paged tables, inline edit, its own dialogs.

    ROW HEIGHT IS ONE NUMBER ACROSS EVERY RECORD LIST — at least 88px, owned by the
    stylesheet, never tightened or padded by a page. Insurance is the reference: its meta
    line carries linked-record pill chips, and a list of plainer records keeps the same
    rhythm so the product scans as one thing.

    The header is the record's IDENTITY — enough to recognise, compare and rule out a
    record without expanding it: type mark, name, state chip, one meta line (ellipsised,
    never wrapped), the sub-collection counts, a headline figure and the row actions.

    The body order is structural, not editorial: Alert → Details → Content → Sections.
    Pass only what the record has; you cannot reorder them, which is the point.

    ONE ACCENT PER RECORD, for what belongs to THIS record. Accent / AccentSoft come from
    the record's TYPE colour — or its type-equivalent: a categorical registry the record
    always has exactly one of (a subscription's billing interval, a budget item's
    category). NEVER derived state (paused / ended / overdue), which stays with the status
    chip. They are set as --rec / --rec-soft on the card, so the header mark, the
    OdsInfoTileGrid icon chips inside Details and any single-series chart inherit the same
    hue with nothing passed down. Omit them and the card falls back to the brand accent
    (tax statements, journal entries, exchange rates have no type). A reference to ANOTHER
    object — a linked account, a contact, a policy, a file type — keeps that object's own
    icon and colour instead, so it stays recognisable as the record it points at.

    ONE CARD OPEN AT A TIME. A record list holds a single open id and drives every card
    controlled — Open="@(_openId == r.Id)", OnToggle="@(o => _openId = o ? r.Id : null)".
    Bodies are unbounded in height, which is only tolerable because opening one record
    closes its siblings.

    Works controlled (Open + OnToggle / @bind-Open) or uncontrolled (DefaultOpen). The
    trigger is a real <button aria-expanded> tied to the body via aria-controls; Actions
    (an OdsMenu, usually) sits OUTSIDE the trigger so it stays independently clickable,
    and the chevron beside it is an aria-hidden mouse affordance with no tab stop of its
    own. Styled by the global .odc-record block in wwwroot/css/odyssey-components.css.
    Parameters and state live in OdsRecordCard.razor.cs.
*@

<div id="@Id" class="@RootClass" style="@AccentStyle">
    <div class="odc-record-head">
        @* The disclosure heading wraps the trigger (the WAI-ARIA accordion pattern) so
           screen readers can navigate a list by heading. display:contents keeps the button
           the grid child it always was, so layout is untouched — and leaves the wrapper
           inert when HeadingLevel is 0 (a div with no role and no box). *@
        <div role="@HeadingRole" aria-level="@HeadingLevelAttr" style="display:contents">
            @* aria-controls names the body only while it exists: the body is not rendered
               when the card is closed, and an IDREF pointing at nothing is invalid. *@
            <button type="button"
                    class="odc-record-trigger"
                    aria-expanded="@(IsOpen ? "true" : "false")"
                    aria-controls="@(IsOpen ? _bodyId : null)"
                    @onclick="ToggleAsync">
                @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(Icon))
                {
                    <span class="odc-record-mark">
                        <span class="material-icons" aria-hidden="true">@Icon</span>
                    </span>
                }
                <span class="odc-record-id">
                    <span class="odc-record-namerow">
                        <span class="odc-record-name">@Name</span>
                        @Chips
                    </span>
                    @if (MetaItems.Count > 0 || CountItems.Count > 0)
                    {
                        <span class="odc-record-meta">
                            @for (var i = 0; i < MetaItems.Count; i++)
                            {
                                @if (i > 0)
                                {
                                    <span class="odc-record-dot" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
                                }
                                <span class="odc-record-metaitem">@MetaItems[i]</span>
                            }
                            @if (CountItems.Count > 0)
                            {
                                @if (MetaItems.Count > 0)
                                {
                                    <span class="odc-record-dot" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
                                }
                                <span class="odc-record-counts">
                                    @foreach (var count in CountItems)
                                    {
                                        <span title="@count.Label">
                                            @if (IsLigature(count.Icon))
                                            {
                                                <span class="material-icons" aria-hidden="true">@count.Icon</span>
                                            }
                                            else
                                            {
                                                <span class="odc-record-glyph" aria-hidden="true">@count.Icon</span>
                                            }
                                            @count.Value
                                            @* The glyph is decorative, so the count would otherwise
                                               reach a screen reader as a bare number. *@
                                            <span class="odc-record-sr">@(" " + count.Label)</span>
                                        </span>
                                    }
                                </span>
                            }
                        </span>
                    }
                </span>
                @if (Figure is not null)
                {
                    <span class="odc-record-figure">
                        <span class="odc-record-value @FigureToneClass">@Figure</span>
                        @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(FigureCaption))
                        {
                            <span class="odc-record-caption">@FigureCaption</span>
                        }
                    </span>
                }
            </button>
        </div>
        @* Row actions are siblings of the trigger, not nested inside it: a menu inside a
           button is invalid, and a click on it must not toggle the card. *@
        <div class="odc-record-ctl" @onclick:stopPropagation="true">
            @Actions
            @* Redundant mouse affordance only — the trigger above is the keyboard control,
               so this adds neither a second tab stop nor a second announcement. *@
            <button type="button" class="odc-record-chev" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" @onclick="ToggleAsync">
                <span class="material-icons">expand_more</span>
            </button>
        </div>
    </div>
    @if (IsOpen)
    {
        <div class="odc-record-body" id="@_bodyId">
            @Alert
            @Details
            @Content
            @Sections
        </div>
    }
</div>
